<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Johann Heinrich Pestalozzi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1746">1746</a>–<a href="/years/1827">1827</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Johann Heinrich Pestalozzi was a Swiss educational reformer born in Zürich. In his novel Leonard and Gertrude and his schools for poor children, most famously at Yverdon from 1805, he argued that teaching should develop the whole child, 'head, heart and hand', through observation and activity. His methods influenced elementary education across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/johann-heinrich-pestalozzi.jpg" alt="Johann Heinrich Pestalozzi" width="320" height="417" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Francisco Javier Ramos · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Johann_Heinrich_Pestalozzi_(Real_Academia_de_Bellas_Artes_de_San_Fernando,_Madrid).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Pestalozzi was born on 12 January 1746 in Zürich, the second of three children. His father, a surgeon and oculist, died when Pestalozzi was five, and the family was supported with the help of their maid, Barbara Schmid. On holidays he visited his grandfather, a clergyman in Höngg, where he learned of the poverty of country peasants.</p><p>He was educated to become a clergyman, but the failure of his first sermon and the influence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-jacques-rousseau">Rousseau</a> led him towards law and politics. At 19 he joined the Helvetic Society, founded by his former teacher <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-jakob-bodmer">Johann Jakob Bodmer</a>. He exposed cases of official corruption and was held under arrest for three days; this made him many political enemies and ended his hopes of a legal career. He then became a farmer, married Anna Schulthess in 1769 and built a house called Neuhof on heavily mortgaged land that proved unsuitable for farming.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>He published the four volumes of Leonard and Gertrude between 1781 and 1787, drawing on his knowledge of peasant life. In it a mother, Gertrude, teaches her children to live morally, and a schoolteacher, a clergyman and a politician follow her example.</p><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serfdom">serfdom</a> was abolished in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a> in 1798 he decided to become an educator. After the French army invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stans">Stans</a> in 1798, the Swiss government recruited him on 5 December to run a new orphanage there; the first orphans arrived on 14 January 1799, in a dreadful condition of body and mind. He then taught at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgdorf">Burgdorf</a> from 1800 and opened the Educational Institute for the Children of the Middle Classes in its castle in October 1800.</p><p>The institute at Yverdon opened in July 1805 and attracted visitors and pupils from all over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, with governments sending educators to study there. Disunion grew among his colleagues, and a state commission in 1810 praised his ideas but not the institute's practices, ending hopes that Yverdon would become a state institution.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Pestalozzi returned to Neuhof and published his Swansong, which provoked bitter replies from Fellenberg and Niederer. He fell ill on 15 February 1827 and died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brugg">Brugg</a> two days later. His methods were child-centred and based on individual differences, sense perception and the pupil's self-activity, and he held that human nature is essentially good. Friedrich Fröbel, the creator of the kindergarten, was his student, and Charles and Elizabeth Mayo founded the formal education of infant teachers in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> after Charles lived with him at Yverdon from 1819 to 1822.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Johann Heinrich Pestalozzi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the eighteenth century the city was a centre of the German-language Enlightenment, home of the critics Johann Jakob Bodmer and Johann Jakob Breitinger, the poet Salomon Gessner and the writer Johann Caspar Lavater, and birthplace of the educator <b>Johann Heinrich Pestalozzi</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/canton-of-zurich">Canton of Zürich · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Zurich, in what is now <a href="/countries/switzerland">Switzerland</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brugg">Brugg</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/switzerland">Switzerland</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=47.37,8.54&amp;year=1798" aria-label="Zurich on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Johann Heinrich Pestalozzi"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5493 6039h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5493 6039h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=47.37,8.54&amp;year=1798">What was here in 1798? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
